  :adr/related "adr-2609062240-argless-itonami-opens-a-start-screen-not-a-help-text adr-2609062400-the-hermes-slash-commands-land-on-the-harness-main-has adr-2609042200"
  :adr/body "## What was asked

Owner instruction, 2026-09-06, against a screenshot of another agent CLI:
reproduce the input area -- a rule above the prompt, a rule below it, and a
status bar under that -- make itonami's theme colour green, and make the input
behave like an editor, so that Up in a multi-line buffer moves to the line
above the way it does in claude cli.

## Why readline had to go

`node:readline/promises` gives one line and owns the row it is on. It cannot
draw a rule above and below itself, and it cannot put a bar under the cursor.
Those two are cosmetic and could have been worked around.

The third could not: readline binds Up to history unconditionally. In a buffer
holding more than one line, Up has to mean the line above first, and the
previous thing I sent only when there is no line above. That is one keystroke
readline will not give up, so the input area is now an editor of this
repository's own: `bin/itonami_editor.cljk`.

## The split, and why the keys are testable

`itonami-editor` is pure. `handle` takes a state and a decoded key and returns
a state; `render` takes a state and a geometry and returns the exact rows plus
where the caret goes. Raw mode, byte reading and cursor motion live in
`bin/itonami` and hold no rules.

That is what lets the key semantics be checked without a TTY -- and the key
semantics are the whole point. The suite runs three controls, each measured:
making Up reach for history unconditionally (what readline does) fails
`up-means-the-line-above-before-it-means-history`; letting the status bar grow
past the terminal fails the overflow check in seven places; not decoding a
bracketed paste fails the paste check.

## What the pure half decides

- **Up** is the line above; only at the top does it reach for history. `:stash`
  holds the draft, so Down past the newest entry gives it back -- browsing must
  not consume what was being written.
- **A trailing backslash and Enter** is a newline. Terminals disagree about
  Shift+Enter and several send a bare Return for it, so a newline reachable
  only through a modifier is unreachable on some of them. Alt/Option+Enter
  works too, anywhere in the line.
- **A bracketed paste is text**, newlines included. Without it a pasted newline
  is the same byte as Return, so pasting three lines SENT the first and dropped
  the other two -- measured here before `ESC[?2004h` was switched on.
- **An escape sequence this does not know is dropped, not typed.** A terminal's
  unsolicited device-attributes reply is `ESC[?1;2c`, and until the CSI pattern
  admitted the private `?` it went into the buffer as `[?1;2c`.
- **Ctrl+C clears the draft; only an already-empty buffer signals.** An
  interrupt that ends the session on the first press is an exit with a
  different name. **Ctrl+D** is EOF only on an empty buffer, and a forward
  delete otherwise.
- The caret is placed in **columns**, not characters, so a line of Japanese
  puts it where the glyphs actually end.

## The status bar says only what is knowable without asking

Profile, how many slash commands, and the keys that work. Nothing on it is a
claim about the server: that would need a request, and a bar that lies once a
second is worse than a bar that says less.

Two states change it: a held card names the tool and the two commands that
answer it, and a run in flight replaces the hints with `esc で中断` -- which is
printed only while ESC really is wired to a stop.

When the terminal is too narrow, whole segments are dropped from the right.
Truncating cut a hint mid-word and left `↑…`, half an instruction; and a bar
wider than the terminal wraps onto the row the caret is about to be moved to,
which tears the frame on the next redraw.

## esc, and what it is allowed to print

ESC during a run posts to `/v1/runs/<id>/stop`. What is printed is what the
server answered -- 中断しました on a 2xx, 中断できません: HTTP n otherwise,
中断を送れません when nothing answered. The first draft printed 中断を要求しました
before the request resolved, which is true of a POST that 404s, and an operator
who reads it stops watching a run that is still going. That is CLAUDE.md's
sixth question in the smallest possible space.

An interrupt the operator asked for returns to the prompt; only a turn that
failed on its own exits 1. Measured with a control: after ESC, `/status` is
accepted and answered, which it could not be if the process had exited.

## Green

The palette moved into the skin, so a skin now carries `:accent-code`,
`:label-code`, `:dim-code` and a five-step `:ramp` for the wordmark alongside
its prompt and banner. `default` is green; `kawaii` and `grok` carry their own;
`gold` is what this wore before the instruction, kept as a skin rather than
deleted, because a palette nobody can get back is a palette nobody can compare.

This also made the theme injection load-bearing in a second place: the splash
plugin reads the active skin at render time, so `/skin gold` changes the
wordmark and not merely the prompt. The test asserts it in both directions.

## itonami_text

`display-width` and its neighbours moved out of `itonami_splash` into
`itonami_text`, which the editor and the splash now share. Two implementations
would have been two opinions about where a border goes, and the second would
have been written by whoever noticed the first was somewhere else. The splash
suite passed unchanged across the move, which is the evidence that it was a
move and not a rewrite.

## What was measured, and how

A TUI cannot be checked by piping bytes at it: readline is gone, the program is
in raw mode, and the output is cursor motion rather than lines. So the checks
run on a real pty of a known size (`pty.fork` + `TIOCSWINSZ`), with a small
terminal emulator to compose what a screen would show.

Confirmed that way, on a live resident: the frame draws and redraws without
tearing at 99 columns; two logical lines display as two rows; Up moves between
them; Ctrl+E and an insert land on the first line; a submitted turn echoes with
its prompt, streams, and returns the frame; ESC stops a counting run and the
stream reports it ending; Japanese input measures correctly on both rows.

One reading was misread on the way: a drive script ending in Ctrl+D showed the
two lines joined, which looked like a wrapping bug and was the documented
forward-delete doing exactly what it should. The raw byte stream settled it.

## What this does not change

`itonami chat \"message\"` still runs one turn and exits. Under a pipe there is
no frame, no colour and no raw mode -- readline still answers there, because
that is the shape it is good at -- and the terminal-restoring escapes are now
guarded, having leaked `[?2004l[?25h` into this file's own pipe test.
"}]
